# .github/workflows/release.yml's TEVI and Pseudoregalia staleness gates hash these files with
# PowerShell's Get-FileHash on a windows-latest runner, and compare against hashes
# dev-scripts/build-tevi.bat / build-pseudoregalia.bat computed locally. GitHub's windows-latest
# runners default to core.autocrlf=true, so without this, actions/checkout silently rewrites
# these LF-committed files to CRLF, changing their hash and permanently failing the gate
# regardless of whether the DLL is actually stale.
adapters/tevi/MeshGhostTevi/*.cs text eol=lf
adapters/tevi/MeshGhostTevi/*.csproj text eol=lf
# The sibling test project is not hashed by any staleness gate, but it is pinned with the
# adapter it compiles by link so the two never disagree about line endings on a Linux runner.
adapters/tevi/MeshGhostTevi.Tests/*.cs text eol=lf
adapters/tevi/MeshGhostTevi.Tests/*.csproj text eol=lf
# The Pseudoregalia harness compiles PeerJson.hpp by include path on a Linux runner, so it is
# pinned LF with the adapter it tests -- same reasoning as the TEVI test project above, and the
# header itself is already covered by the Mod/src/*.hpp rule below.
adapters/pseudoregalia/MeshGhostPseudo.Tests/*.cpp text eol=lf
adapters/pseudoregalia/MeshGhostPseudo/Mod/src/*.cpp text eol=lf
adapters/pseudoregalia/MeshGhostPseudo/Mod/src/*.hpp text eol=lf
adapters/pseudoregalia/MeshGhostPseudo/Mod/CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf

# Shell scripts are pinned LF, and this rule is load-bearing right now, not hypothetical:
# .github/workflows/ci.yml's fuzz job runs `bash dev-scripts/ci-fuzz.sh` on ubuntu-latest, so a
# CRLF copy of it breaks CI outright with the classic "$'\r': command not found". This machine
# CANNOT demonstrate that failure -- Git Bash's sh tolerates CRLF happily (checked 2026-08-16) --
# so a broken script looks perfectly healthy in local testing and only fails on the runner.
# (The release's Linux/macOS packages are tarred on a Linux runner, which keeps the executable
# bit; that is why no chmod helper is needed, not why this rule exists.)
*.sh text eol=lf

# go.mod/go.sum are pinned LF because `go mod tidy` rewrites them wholesale to LF: without this
# they sit in the working tree as CRLF (go.mod was in fact stored MIXED), so a routine tidy
# produces a whole-file phantom diff that looks like a dependency change and is not.
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf

# *.go pinned LF for the same reason, found 2026-08-23. `core.autocrlf=true` means git WRITES CRLF
# whenever it materializes a file, so any revert of a .go file (`git checkout --`, a reverted patch,
# a stash pop) silently re-creates it with CRLF -- with the CONTENT identical to HEAD, so `git
# status` and `git diff` both show nothing and there is no edit to blame. `gofmt -l` then reports
# the file as unformatted and prints a whole-file diff, which reads like a formatting disaster
# rather than a line-ending one, and `dev-scripts/preflight.ps1`'s first check fails.
#
# Four files were sitting in exactly that state when this was added: `core.go`, `bridge.go` and
# both `cmd/*/main.go`, three of them byte-identical to HEAD. The pin is what `go.mod`/`go.sum`
# above already had; `.go` was simply missed.
*.go text eol=lf

# The pre-commit hook is a shell script with no .sh extension, so the rule above does not reach it.
# Same reasoning, and the same failure: a CRLF hook on a Linux or macOS clone dies with the classic
# `$'\r': command not found` and the leak check it performs silently stops running -- which is worse
# than the hook not existing, because preflight would report core.hooksPath as correctly set.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# The player-facing text that ships INSIDE the release zip, pinned to CRLF.
#
# These are opened by double-clicking on Windows, where a file with bare LF
# renders as one endless line in Notepad. They used to survive on the raw bytes
# in the blob rather than on a rule, which held only until something rewrote one
# -- a doc edit on 2026-09-06 did exactly that and flipped README.txt to LF.
# Pinned here instead, so the ending is decided by the repo and not by whichever
# runner happens to check the tree out.
#
# Scoped to the READMEs WE write, deliberately: a wider glob also swept the vendored
# UE4SS runtime's own LICENSE and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt, and a vendored file stays
# byte-identical to what its project shipped.
#
# `**` and not `*` in the games rule, corrected 2026-09-08. A gitattributes `*` does not
# cross a `/`, so `games/*/README.txt` reached tevi/ and pseudoregalia/ and MISSED both
# Pokemon READMEs, which sit a level deeper under games/pokemon/. `git check-attr eol` on
# those two printed `unspecified`: they were CRLF only because the bytes in the blob
# happened to be, which is precisely the state the paragraph above records going wrong on
# 2026-09-06. Check the fix the same way rather than by reading the pattern -- all four
# paths must print `eol: crlf`.
#
# `**` also reaches one file the old `*` never did and that IS wanted: the explanatory
# README.txt we ship inside the user's ue4ss\Mods\ folder, which is our text, opened by
# double-click on Windows exactly like the other four. It is LF in the blob today, so the
# first checkout after this rule lands rewrites it -- a whole-file ending diff with no
# content change, which is the pin doing its job and not something to revert. The vendored
# UE4SS files in that same tree are untouched: they are LICENSE and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt,
# and this glob only matches the basename README.txt.
packaging/release/README.txt text eol=crlf
packaging/release/games/**/README.txt text eol=crlf

# The Linux and macOS READMEs are the same player-facing text for the OTHER packages, and
# they are pinned the opposite way for the same reason: release.yml renames each into the
# tarball as README.txt, where a stray CR is what shows up as a visible ^M in a terminal
# pager. They were unpinned until 2026-09-08 and are LF today only on the blob's bytes.
packaging/unix/README-*.txt text eol=lf

# config.json is the one shipped file that goes to ALL THREE packages from one blob -- the
# Windows zip root, both tarballs, and (cut down by dev-scripts/stage-release.ps1) every
# game folder. LF rather than CRLF deliberately: encoding/json does not care on either
# side, and the two tarballs are the copies where the ending is visible to a reader. Pinned
# 2026-09-08 so it stays decided here rather than by whichever runner checks the tree out.
packaging/release/config.json text eol=lf

# Batch files, for the same reason one line up and with the same history.
#
# `dev-scripts/run-core.bat` states the requirement in its own header: "This file MUST keep CRLF
# line endings. cmd.exe mis-parses labels and `goto` in an LF-only .bat, and the first draft ran
# straight past its own argument validation and launched a core with an empty -game" (2026-08-25).
# That was recorded and then protected by nothing: no rule covered *.bat, so the property lived in
# whichever bytes happened to be in each blob. On 2026-09-07 a one-word sed edit to a COMMENT in
# that same file rewrote every line ending in it and stored it as LF -- a one-line content diff, a
# green tree, and no gate anywhere that could see it.
#
# Pinned for every .bat rather than the three that use labels today, because "does this script use
# goto yet" is not a property anyone will re-check before adding one.
*.bat text eol=crlf

